{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792329640,"nonce":"dD2YkOq48oVq8dXbTCG0jtdSPWs=","title":"In a page the car is not drawn until its textures are compressed, seconds after the rest","message":"The user: \"the car should not even take so long to load, it's a small model\".\n\n**What happens**\n\nA mesh whose texture is still being compressed is not drawn. Compression is Haxe running in wasm on a worker thread: with the banner's two 4096 by 4096 textures it finished 3.2 seconds after the page was up, and until then the car was missing from a window that otherwise looked done: the floor, the rings and the callouts, and no car. The model itself decoded in 0.3 s.\n\n**Fix**\n\nA page uploads textures as they are, in the first frame that asks for them, so a mesh is drawn at once: `MeshTextures.compress` is off in a build defined `wasm`. The textures cost four times the GPU memory, which the banner's web build makes up by loading its two at 2048 by 2048, enough for a window a few thousand pixels wide, so the car is drawn with the page, 0.6 s after the program says it is ready.\n\nThe class's doc said textures were drawn at once and compressed afterwards; it now says what the code does.","files":null}]}